   "en": {
    "headline": "Users say Humble Bundle's new website lets them log in without 2FA and blame vibe coding",
    "summary_short": "Humble Bundle users report that its new website no longer asks for two-factor authentication (2FA) and has lost part of their purchase history. Many blame vibe coding, though no one has offered proof.",
    "summary_long": "Humble Bundle, the store for bundles of digital games and books, has redesigned its website. A widely discussed Hacker News thread collects its users' complaints. According to them, the new site no longer asks for two-factor authentication (2FA) and lets you change your password without entering the old one. Others cannot log in or are missing part of their purchase history. The site itself says the history is still being migrated and that purchases are safe.\n\nThe thread blames vibe coding, that is, writing software by asking an AI for code without reviewing it. No one has offered proof that the site was built this way, and several commenters point out that insecure software predates AI. Others blame whoever uses the tool without knowing how to build software, not the tool itself.",
    "unconfirmed_claims": [
     "Humble Bundle's new website does not ask for two-factor authentication (2FA) at login.",
     "The new website lets users change their password without entering the old one.",
     "The new website was built with vibe coding."
    ]
   },
